# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/18-the-vote-conviction-does-not-take.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # all substantive pins and formal contradiction checks
# Markdown consistency is reviewed separately; these pins do not certify prose.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closes the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact aborts the run rather than loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

# Chapter 12's "Speaking for conditions nobody may spend away" is grounded by
# the ecology guardian/ stateful cases, historical-finality cases, independent
# appointment and alternate-function cases, and
# animal-guardian-conflict/positive. Actual positive records and each
# required-field/upstream omission are separate ordinary cases.
:expect-pins 31

# This chapter says nothing about these two offices; the queries check a claim
# of chapter 12. FSBOD_22 is the Future Conditions Guardian and FSBOD_23 the
# Animal Protection Advocate. No record for either is supplied here, so
# neither the Guardian's automatic stay nor the Advocate's inspection
# permission follows: no stay or inspection power arises from an office's
# name alone.
? interrupt(FSBOD_22, ECReaderStay, ECGuardianAutomaticStay).
# => FALSE
? permits(FSBOD_23, ECOnlyExactLawfullyAccessibleAnimalInspection, ECReaderInspection).
# => FALSE

# "In Hano's case the rules conclude confinement and a ballot entitlement
# together."
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

# Free movement needs a person who is not a prisoner, so it does not follow
# for Hano, while the ballot conclusion still does: "Hano loses free movement
# and stays part of the electorate".
? travel(Hano).
# => FALSE

? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# Jala, an adult nobody holds in custody, reaches the ballot by the same
# route as Hano: "The basic ballot entitlement follows from personhood and
# recorded general adulthood."
? decide(Jala, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# "Conviction withdraws free movement and leaves the person's other rights
# where they were", checked item by item.
? person(Hano).
# => TRUE

# Hano is owed expression as a floor item, and the record shows no act of it:
# "custody itself produces no recorded act of expression".
? expresses(Hano).
# => FALSE

# No credibility finding and no loss of recognition follow from the case:
# "The case leaves Hano's credibility untouched".
? false(Hano).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Hano).
# => FALSE

# A credibility finding does not reach the franchise either: "Bela is under a
# credibility finding and still holds the ballot conclusion." The pair must
# sit adjacent: the TRUE franchise conclusion only carries the section's claim
# while the finding stands beside it.
? false(Bela).
# => TRUE

? decide(Bela, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# Cira is a person and the supplied record lacks typed evidence that the common
# adulthood threshold was reached. The FALSE results establish only the bounded
# executable answer: they do not establish Cira's outside age or legal status.
? person(Cira).
# => TRUE

? prevents(Cira, GeneralAdulthoodDenial).
# => FALSE

? decide(Cira, Ballot).
# => FALSE

# A narrower ballot rule, giving the vote only to adults outside custody, is
# accepted and then removed again: the refusal that guards the floor does not
# guard the ballot, and "that refusal protects the floor alone". The ballot
# rule "can still be narrowed or replaced", and "a rule the computer accepts
# is not thereby a lawful amendment". If the franchise gains the floor's
# protection, this pin becomes a refusal and the chapter must change with it.
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & match($x, GeneralAdult) & ~prisoner($x) -> decide($x, Ballot).

# ── MISSING EVIDENCE LOOKS LIKE NOT YET. Ground facts below persist ──────────
# Esa teaches, was examined, and is somebody's recorded child. None proves an
# age. Without typed adulthood evidence the bounded model derives neither the
# anti-denial wall nor the franchise conclusion. Supplying that evidence makes
# both derive; it does not constitute adulthood or prove an outside clock. The
# pair is self-controlling: FALSE holds before the supplied evidence and TRUE
# after it.
? prevents(Esa, GeneralAdulthoodDenial).
# => FALSE

? decide(Esa, Ballot).
# => FALSE

# Enter typed adulthood evidence for Esa, then ask the same questions again.
at(Esa, GeneralAdulthood).

? prevents(Esa, GeneralAdulthoodDenial).
# => TRUE

? decide(Esa, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# `decide` is conclusion-only: the rules may conclude a ballot entitlement,
# and no entry may write one. The admission below stays resident, so the name
# is admitted vocabulary while the forged conclusion is refused; the block
# sits near the end of the file so that only the final blocks run with it.
:accept
admits("decide").

# Even with its name admitted, "a person entry submitted together with a
# forged entitlement is refused along with it": the whole entry rolls back,
# so the otherwise lawful person fact does not stay either.
:refuse reasoning /`decide` is declared derived-only/
person(Vote_Probe) & decide(Vote_Probe, Ballot).

? person(Vote_Probe).
# => FALSE

# An entry giving the ballot with an extra place, naming an election, is
# refused in the same way, beside Hano's two-place conclusion above.
:refuse reasoning /`decide` is declared derived-only/
decide(Ghost, Ballot, Election).

# ── THE NARROWER RULE, LEFT RESIDENT. THIS ACCEPT MUST STAY UNSCOPED ─────────
# The scoped accept above proves the clause LOADS and then puts the base back,
# so it cannot show what the chapter says here: "An additional grant has not
# repealed the existing entitlement". This one leaves the clause RESIDENT, and
# the query underneath is that sentence: with the narrower ballot rule in
# force, Hano's ballot "still follows from the broader rule", because an extra
# sufficient route removes nothing.
#
# DO NOT EXPECT THE QUERY TO DEPEND ON THE ACCEPT. Measured both ways: drop the
# accept and the verdict is identical, because the ballot conclusion comes
# from the wider rule. That identity IS the pinned claim — the clause is in
# force and changes no answer — the same shape as the no-dead-conjuncts
# fixture, where nothing moving is the result. What this block adds over the
# scoped accept above is the word RESIDENT: the hostile clause is not merely
# loadable, it is standing in the record while the answer stays. An accepted
# rule stays in the KB, so every pin below runs with it; it concludes nothing
# about the child those pins ask after.
:accept
all $x: person($x) & match($x, GeneralAdult) & ~prisoner($x) -> decide($x, Ballot).

? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# ── The child with nobody ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "Nell's birth entry holds nothing about general adulthood, so the ballot
# conclusion is absent for Nell."
? decide(Nell, Ballot).
# => FALSE

? at(Nell, GeneralAdulthood).
# => FALSE

# "Either way the absence is no taking: custody and a credibility finding are
# both absent for Nell".
? prisoner(Nell).
# => FALSE

? false(Nell).
# => FALSE

# "Nell already holds the right to be heard: the prohibition on a minimum
# speaking age follows from personhood alone."
? prevents(Nell, ChildVoiceNoAgeFloor).
# => TRUE
